FetchRow answers from the store for a row the view has trimmed

The four steps `R19.2b-fetchrow-and-the-gate-loop.md` wrote down, done — this is the item
the gate loop had deferred, and the loop is gone.

## What was wrong

`FetchRow` exists so a head can read a row its daemon is not holding. It could not: the
server answered through `hub.row_body_at`, which reads the **bounded view** — 2,000 rows,
8 MB of bodies — and a snapshot's `items` *is* that same vector. So a trimmed ordinal
answered `null` and a carried one was a row the head already had. leticl measured it; the
seam already draws three states and one of them, *"the daemon does not hold them any more"*,
was unreachable and had nothing true to say.

The protocol's own doc had already settled the shape it should have: *"the daemon is a
**proxy** for anything its own caches do not hold… so a head asks once and never learns
which tier answered."*

## The seam, and it is the `SessionSource` pattern rather than a new one

- **`registry::RowSource`** — `fn row_body(&self, session_id, row) -> Option<String>` —
  beside `SessionSource`, set by `Registry::set_row_source` exactly as `source` is set by
  `set_source` (*"set once at startup by the daemon"*), and `None` in every head and every
  test that predates it, where behaviour is exactly what it was.
- **On the registry and not on each hub.** The server's `FetchRow` arm already holds the
  registry, and the registry is what knows whether there is a store — so no hub grows a
  field, no hub-construction site changes, and there is no fourth place for a hub to be
  built without it.
- **`Registry::row_body_from_store`** clones the `Arc` out before calling it, so the lock
  is not held across a SQLite read: a head paging a trimmed row cannot block a listing.
- **The server asks the view first, then the store** — one lock and a `Vec` index for the
  fast path, and the store only for a row the view has trimmed. The clamping, the cap and
  the character-boundary arithmetic are untouched.

## The store's reads, which are one indexed lookup each

- `Store::row_json_at(transcript_id, seq)` — `transcript_item`'s primary key is
  `(transcript_id, seq)`, so this is **not** a scan and **not** `load_transcript`, which
  reads every row of the session to return one. That is the whole difference the frame
  needs.
- `Store::current_transcript_id(session_id)` — the **newest** transcript, ordered
  `created_at DESC, rowid DESC` (the rowid because a fork is written in the same
  millisecond as the row that caused it). After a compaction, ordinal 3 is a row of the
  *new* base; reading the old transcript's row 3 would answer with a row nobody asked
  about.

`harnessd`'s `StoreSessions` implements `RowSource` — **the same instance** it already
gives `set_source`, because that is one store asked two questions and a third connection
would be a third thing in WAL's reader set. The wiring is two lines in `bin/harnessd.rs`,
beside the `set_source` it mirrors.

## `body_of`, one definition

A row's body is a match over the item kind (a payload, a text, a user row's text parts
joined, a segment mark's nothing) and it now lives **once**, in
`letibot_sessionlog::body_of`, called by the view's reader *and* by the store's. Two copies
is how the two tiers would come to disagree about what `body` means. The *windowing* is
deliberately not in it: the cap and the boundary arithmetic belong to whoever answers the
frame.

## Tests

- `store::a_row_is_readable_by_ordinal_without_loading_its_transcript` — the row by
  ordinal through the same serde form the wire carries; `None` (not `""`) for an ordinal
  the transcript does not have; the session→current-transcript mapping the glue depends on;
  and `None` for a session nothing was written for.
- `store::an_ordinal_follows_the_fork_to_the_new_base` — after `put_fork`, the *new* base
  is what an ordinal is relative to, and its row 0 is not the old transcript's.
- `view::a_rows_body_is_its_own_kind_of_text` — the shared definition pinned per variant,
  rather than inferred from whichever tier a test happened to exercise.
- The existing `sessionlog/tests/fetch_row.rs` (8 tests) is unchanged and still passes: with
  no row source installed, the behaviour is exactly what it was.

```
cargo test -p letibot-tools       613 passed, 0 failed
cargo test -p letibot-tui         317 passed, 0 failed, 4 ignored
cargo test -p letibot-harnessd --lib 114 passed, 0 failed
cargo test -p letibot-ui          103 passed, 0 failed
cargo test -p letibot-sessionlog   96 passed, 0 failed
cargo test -p letibot-tokencore    61 passed, 0 failed
```

**Not verified, and it is the same limit as `da4a576`:** the daemon running this session
was built before this commit, so **no head has yet fetched a trimmed row through it**. What
is proven is the unit — the store read, the registry's second tier, and the server's arm
asking the view first. The end-to-end proof is on the next restart, and the head half
already draws the state it will then have something true to say in.

`rustfmt --check` still cannot run (it writes a temp file, which the gate blocks).
